  "_measured": "production api.bainluck.com via admin db-query, 2026-08-30 22:22:30 UTC. `_measured_at` is the DATABASE's own clock, read in the same batch, and it is the anchor every date below is relative to.",
  "_query": "the exact corpus TennisEventAdapter.build_event sees: llm_sport_category='tennis' AND (status='open' OR (status IN ('resolved','closed','settled') AND resolution_date >= now() - 30 days)), narrowed in SQL to winner-shaped non-matchup names and re-filtered in Python with the real is_winner_market regex (28 in, 28 kept). real_outcome_count is computed from the real futures_outcomes names with the real is_field_outcome / is_placeholder_outcome_name helpers, never from a bare row count. Outcomes fetched in four market_id %% 4 chunks, none truncated.",
  "_why": "UX-P202 / CERT-500 [P1]. The 2026-08-12 corpus (tennis_production_corpus.json) carries NO resolution_date at all, so no date-shaped tennis guard had a real-data witness and the rail/detail agreement test had to invent one — which is precisely why it could not see the bug (UX-P200-7). This corpus carries the real dates and it carries the defect: THREE tournaments (Montreal, Toronto, Cincinnati) are rendered by one source with the FULLEST draw and NO date, and by another with a smaller draw and a real date. It also carries the stale rendering that makes the past-date rule load-bearing: `WTA Cincinnati Winner` exists twice, open and resolving 2026-09-22, and resolved and dated 2026-08-25.",
  "_how_to_use": "Dates are FIXED calendar instants and would go stale (gotcha #44): shift every resolution_date by (now - _measured_at) so the corpus's RELATIVE structure — which is what any guard here is about — is preserved without a clock branch. Offset first, never an `if`.",
